CameraRig.description:
The rig on the camera entity. `rig` is the authored configuration (writing a different value
starts a timed blend), `shake` survives switches, `trauma`, `grounded` and `look_deg` are
game-written inputs, `state` is engine-written. The kick, roll and FOV channels are game-written
too (the engine decays only the two kicks) and apply after aim. Every field defaults, so older documents keep loading.

# Example

```
use ludion_camera::{CameraRig, RigMode};

let rig: CameraRig = serde_json::from_value(serde_json::json!({
    "rig": { "mode": { "track": { "path": "paths/behind-1.path.yaml", "offset": [0.0, 2.2, 4.5] } } },
    "trauma": 0.25,
}))
.unwrap();
let RigMode::Track(track) = &rig.rig.mode else { unreachable!() };
assert_eq!(track.offset, [0.0, 2.2, 4.5]);
assert_eq!(rig.rig.springs.position_half_life, 0.15);
```

look_deg.description:
Look input `[yaw, pitch]` in degrees (positive yaw turns left, positive pitch looks down)
for `first_person` and `chase`; `follow` consumes and discards it. The game writes it,
usually through [`add_look`]; the rig applies and zeroes it on each tick it poses.

ContactMaterial.description:
The contact material of a `static` or `kinematic` collider: friction, restitution and their
combine rules. A [`Body::Rigid`] body carries the same four fields on [`RigidBody`].

```yaml
body: { static: { restitution: 0.3 } }
body: { kinematic: { friction_combine: max } }
```

LaneConstraint.description:
Holds a [`CharacterMotor`](ludion_physics::CharacterMotor) to a `kind: path` document: the
character's displacement is projected onto the path's horizontal direction and a pull keeps it
on the centre line, eased by `blend`. An entity needs a `CharacterMotor` for the lane to be read
(`L1302`).

`s` and `blend` are the lane's own state, written by `move_character`; a game writes `engaged`.
Changing `path` at runtime must also null `s`: use [`set_path`](Self::set_path).

# Example
```
use ludion_components::{LaneConstraint, LaneEnds};

let lane: LaneConstraint = serde_json::from_value(serde_json::json!({
    "path": "paths/corridor.path.yaml",
    "engaged": true,
    "ends": "stop",
}))
.unwrap();
assert!(lane.engaged);
assert_eq!(lane.ends, LaneEnds::Stop);
assert_eq!(lane.s, None);
```

PathFollower.description:
Moves an entity along a `kind: path` document: `path_follow` writes its `Transform.translation`
every tick (scale is untouched, and so is rotation unless `orient` is set), advancing `phase` by `speed` on gameplay time.

The pose is in the parent's frame — the path's points are scene coordinates for a root and local
to the `Parent` otherwise. A game drives the position
itself by writing `phase` with `speed: 0`; the write poses on the next tick.
`path_follower_motion` reads what the tick did, and a wrap or jump is a teleport for a
kinematic body.

# Example
```
use ludion_components::{PathFollower, PathMode, PathOrient};

let lift: PathFollower = serde_json::from_value(serde_json::json!({
    "path": "paths/lift.path.yaml",
    "speed": 2.0,
    "mode": "ping_pong",
}))
.unwrap();
assert_eq!(lift.mode, PathMode::PingPong);
assert_eq!(lift.phase, 0.0);
assert_eq!(lift.orient, PathOrient::None);
```

PatrolFollower.description:
An entity that patrols a `kind: patrol` document: `patrol_update` writes its `NavGoal` for each
stop in turn, so every leg is a nav path around obstacles. The entity also needs a `NavAgent`.
`PatrolState` is the state `patrol_update` keeps; the game reads the facing from it (or from
`patrol_motion`) and turns the body itself.

While `paused` is true the patrol leaves the guard alone (the game or a behaviour drives it); when
it turns off the guard rejoins by `resume`. `route` must be a relative path without `..` (`L1524`).

```yaml
components:
  NavAgent: {}
  PatrolFollower:
    route: patrols/yard.patrol.yaml
    resume: nearest
```

```
use ludion_components::{PatrolFollower, PatrolResume};

let guard: PatrolFollower =
    serde_json::from_value(serde_json::json!({ "route": "patrols/yard.patrol.yaml" })).unwrap();
assert_eq!(guard.resume, PatrolResume::Nearest);
assert!(!guard.paused);
```

Look.description:
A facing offset from the entity's own rotation, in degrees. Yaw 0 looks along the entity's −Z,
**positive yaw turns left** and **positive pitch looks down**: the camera rig's convention.

```
use ludion_components::Look;

let look: Look = serde_json::from_value(serde_json::json!({ "pitch_deg": 10.0 })).unwrap();
assert_eq!(look, Look { yaw_deg: 0.0, pitch_deg: 10.0 });
```

SightZone.description:
One wedge of sight: a range, horizontal and vertical half-angles, and the classes it detects. A
point is in the zone when its horizontal and vertical angles are inside the half-angles and its
distance from the eye is within the effective range (a wedge, not a cone).

```
use ludion_components::SightZone;

let zone: SightZone = serde_json::from_value(serde_json::json!({
    "name": "inner", "range": 6.0, "half_angle_deg": 60.0,
    "detects": ["player"], "see_through": ["cover_overhead"], "see_through_max_range": 3.0,
}))
.unwrap();
assert_eq!(zone.vertical_half_angle_deg, 45.0);
assert!(!zone.range_by_visibility);
```

LayerSet.description:
An ordered list of layer names, in the author's order (never sorted, so a scene's text does not
churn). Serializes as a plain list of strings.

```
use ludion_physics::LayerSet;

let set = LayerSet::from(vec!["light_occluder".to_string()]);
assert_eq!(set.as_slice(), ["light_occluder"]);
assert!(!set.is_empty());
assert_eq!(serde_json::to_string(&set).unwrap(), r#"["light_occluder"]"#);
```

ZoneSweep.description:
A closed-form yaw sweep of a [`Perceiver`]'s zones: a camera or searchlight panning to and fro.
It is a pure function of gameplay time (a held `GameClock` holds it).

```
use ludion_components::{SweepShape, ZoneSweep};

let sweep: ZoneSweep = serde_json::from_value(serde_json::json!({
    "amplitude_deg": 40.0, "period_s": 8.0, "dwell_s": 1.0,
}))
.unwrap();
assert_eq!(sweep.shape, SweepShape::PingPongLinear);
assert_eq!(sweep.phase_s, 0.0);
```

